<CENTER><IMG SRC="img/goldbar3.gif" WIDTH=580 HEIGHT=3 ALIGN="BOTTOM"></CENTER>

<BR>
<FONT SIZE=6>
<CENTER>
Unlimited Hydroplanes
</FONT>
</CENTER>


<FONT SIZE=4>

<P>
Unlimited Hydroplanes are the top of the line among the fastest boats in the world.  Skimming
across the water at speeds up to 200 miles-per-hour, these craft are spectacular to
watch.  

<P>
<CENTER>
<IMG SRC="img/anatomy5.gif" ALT="[Anatomy Pic1]" WIDTH=500 HEIGHT=211 ALIGN="BOTTOM">
</CENTER>

<FONT SIZE=-1>
<CENTER>(Photos and 'anatomy' copyright Budweiser and the UHRA)</CENTER>
</FONT>

<P>
The Unlimiteds are built using aircraft construction techniques, and each boat is one of a
kind.  From the 1950's through the 70's and into the 80's, World War II aircraft engines
were the powerplant of choice.  Lately, helicopter turbine engines are used by all but one
team, the father/son owned Cooper U-3 which continues to set piston-powered lap speed
records.
<P>
Once a hydroplane comes up to speed, it "hydroplanes" on top of the water using
airflow beneath the boat to decrease the boat's drag on the water.  Hydroplanes
fly more than they float, with the perfect 'stance' being just half of the
prop in the water, and a square foot or so of water contact on the forward
sponsons.  Sometimes a boat will catch too much air and do a breath-taking
backflip.
<P>
Recent improvements in the safety of Unlimited Hydroplanes have saved the
life of more than one driver.  The most notable advancement has been the
use of modified military aircraft cockpits to secure the pilot in a watertight
capsule.  While the cockpit itself has not survived all crashes, the
pilots have.  Detroit's Gold Cup volunteer rescue crews are the finest in
the world, and their practices are being refined and put to use at all the
other race sites.
<P>
One of the few regulations of Unlimited Hydroplane Racing (outside of safety
issues) is that the boat must be propeller driven.  The large pipe coming off
the jet engine towards the tail is used only as an exhaust.  Within the last
few years a fuel regulator rule has also been in place, and it is always a
source of controversy.  Purists feel the rule has no place in an 'Unlimited'
class, while the realists counter that parts for these out-of-manufacture
engines are very limited in availability.

</FONT>

<P>
<FONT SIZE=-1>
(Text copyright Brian Reed)
</FONT>


<P>
<CENTER><IMG SRC="img/goldbar3.gif" WIDTH=580 HEIGHT=3 ALIGN="BOTTOM"></CENTER>

<P>
<FONT SIZE=6>
<CENTER>
Unlimited Anatomy
</CENTER>
</FONT>

<P>
<CENTER>
<IMG SRC="img/anatomy1.gif" ALT="[Anatomy Pic2]" WIDTH=320 HEIGHT=204 ALIGN="BOTTOM">
</CENTER>

<FONT SIZE=-1>
<CENTER>(See also the photo at the top of this page)</CENTER>
</FONT>


<FONT SIZE=4>

<P>
<OL>
<LI><B>Airtrap</B> - An extension of the inside wall that forms the boats tunnel.  Changing
the length of the airtrap changes the hull's ride.
<LI><B>Cockpit</B> - The ultimate in captain's chairs.  This is a modified, watertight
jet aircraft canopy.
<LI><B>Deck</B> - The upper surface of the hull.
<LI><B>Engine</B> - The motor that drives the boat, usually a Lycoming turbine.
<LI><B>Air intake</B> - Covers the engine and improves the hull aerodynamics.
<LI><B>Nontrip chine</B> - The vertical surface of the hull that is inclined to aid in cornering.
<LI><B>Nose Cowl</B> - Its aerodynamic shape deflects the air around the cockpit and
engine compartment.
<LI><B>Propeller</B> - Also called a 'wheel' or 'prop'.  The propeller is the only force that
moves the boat.  It may have 2 or 3 blades, and is usually less than 10 inches in diameter.
<LI><B>Prop shaft</B> - Transmits power from the engine back to the propeller.
<LI><B>Rudder</B> - A blade near the transom that sticks into the water and turns the boat.
<LI><B>Runner</B> - The surface that touches the water at the bottom of each sponson.
<LI><B>Shaft Log</B> - The fitting in the bottom of the hull through which the prop shaft passes.
<LI><B>Skid Fin</B> - The blade attached to the left sponson.  It keeps the boat from sliding
to the right during a turn.
<LI><B>Slats</B> - Adjustable flaps on the leading edge of the deck.  They change the airflow
over and under the hull, which in turn changes lift.
<LI><B>Sponsons</B> - The two pontoon-like parts of the front of the hull.
<LI><B>Strut</B> - The rear bearing for the prop shaft.
<LI><B>Tail Fin</B> - The vertical pieces that support the wing and provide directional stability.
<LI><B>Transom</B> - The vertical surface on the rear of the hull.
<LI><B>Tunnel</B> - The space beneath the hull proper and the sponsons and airtraps.  This
constrains a cushion of air on which a hydroplane travels.
<LI><B>Wing</B> - The horizontal airfoil that helps stabilize a boats attitude.
</OL>

</FONT>


